      "writtenOn": "2026-09-08T19:55:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why is this symbol needed for db845? Is this going to break other partners?",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "52829eae_70ebc20e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1865564
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-09T02:36:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Without this patch, presubmit failed with:\n\n```\nThese symbols are missing from the symbol list and are not available at runtime for unsigned modules:\n  watchdog_notify_pretimeout required by [\u0027qcom-wdt.ko\u0027, \u0027pm8916_wdt.ko\u0027]\n```",
      "parentUuid": "644336da_76e47841",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dcc78af2_3eabadc3",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-10T16:20:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If it broke db845, it will likely break other partner drivers. Something in this series caused watchdog_notify_pretimeout to be required. What will happen if this GKI binary is used with drivers that are not rebuilt with these changes? Will they behave correctly?",
      "parentUuid": "52829eae_70ebc20e",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "11b4a2cc_7f7f2af3",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1865564
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T01:29:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Before the series, watchdog_notify_pretimeout() is an inline function [1].\n\nAfter enabling CONFIG_WATCHDOG_PRETIMEOUT_GOV\u003dy (via ANDROID: gki_defconfig: Enable \"dump\" pretimeout governor), watchdog_notify_pretimeout()[2] becomes an exported symbol.  qcom-wdt.c relies on the symbol[3].\n\nWithout this patch, it seems the symbol will be \"trimmed\".  As a result, qcom-wdt.c can\u0027t find the symbol.\n\nTodd: Is this common in GKI?  I\u0027d like to learn what is the process to handle this situation.\n\n[1] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/include/linux/watchdog.h#L208\n[2] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_pretimeout.c#L113\n[3] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c#L69",
      "parentUuid": "dcc78af2_3eabadc3",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0bda11e7_7b61b5e0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T22:10:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is not common, but this sort of thing comes up occasionally. Somebody needing to change db845 or pixel symbols/drivers is kind of a red flag that we might be introducing breakage if the GKI kernel is replaced without rebuilding all vendor modules.\n\nSo what will happen in this case if the db845 module is not rebuilt for this change? I\u0027m guessing that this watchdog feature will not work properly. If that is true, the next question is whether it is possible to refactor the feature a little so that the old inline function still works.",
      "parentUuid": "11b4a2cc_7f7f2af3",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b22dda8c_b69ec7d7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1865564
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-12T04:36:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I missed your question:\n\n\u003e What will happen if this GKI binary is used with drivers that are not rebuilt with these changes? Will they behave correctly?\n\nThe modules will call into the inline one instead of GKI binary one of watchdog_notify_pretimeout().  It works without breaking things, but incorrectly (from \"dump\" pretimeout governor\u0027s perspective).\n\n---\n\n\u003e Is this common in GKI?\n\nTo provide more context, I had the impression because there was also a similar case recently, https://r.android.com/4129114, the arch_touch_nmi_watchdog() becomes a exported symbol after enabling CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR\u003dy [4].  And I would like to learn more experience about how to deal with it to make the review smooth in the future.\n\n[4] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/include/linux/nmi.h#L91\n\n---\n\n\u003e So what will happen in this case if the db845 module is not rebuilt for this change?\n\nThe \"dump\" pretimeout governor will not work correctly.  The db845 modules should still call into the inline watchdog_notify_pretimeout() which only prints a line of log.\n\n---\n\n\u003e If that is true, the next question is whether it is possible to refactor the feature a little so that the old inline function still works.\n\nI think the old inline function still works even if they don\u0027t rebuild with the new GKI binary.  However, we need to modify gki/aarch64/symbols/db845c here to make new modules build with the exported watchdog_notify_pretimeout().\n\n---\n\nI guess we are lucky here that the old inline function just works.  I would like to learn more about \"refactor\" if it isn\u0027t the case: do you mean we would need to refactor in Linux upstream kernel to make it compliant to GKI (specifically, old modules should work with new GKI binary) before we can land it in GKI?",
      "parentUuid": "0bda11e7_7b61b5e0",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fab4bd8c_42a9b044",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-16T00:19:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"refactor\" in this context would mean modifying the patch when it is cherry-picked to ACK (not upstream) so that drivers that aren\u0027t rebuilt still work properly.\n\nI can\u0027t tell from your response whether the refactor is needed. You say \"The \"dump\" pretimeout governor will not work correctly\", but you also way \"we are lucky that the old inline function just works\". Does this mean the watchdog functionality that the db845 does not work? Or does it work as expected, but lacks extra functionality that this patch provides?\n\nIf the feature works as intended for db845 (but lacks some extra features provided by this patch), then I\u0027m fine to go ahead with this. I\u0027m not actually worried about db845, but I\u0027m worried about other partners who might be relying on this behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "b22dda8c_b69ec7d7",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "27e18463_af33831d",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1865564
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-16T01:24:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If it is an old module that isn\u0027t rebuilt with the new GKI binary, its watchdog_notify_pretimeout() is still the inline function [1] (which just prints a line of log).  It will continue to work as expected, it just won\u0027t have the new pretimeout governor features.\n\nFor modules that are rebuilt, their watchdog_notify_pretimeout() will use the newly exported function [2] and gain the new functionality (which prints all CPU backtraces).\n\nFrom my best understanding, there should be no breakage for other partners who don\u0027t rebuild their modules.\n\n[1] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/include/linux/watchdog.h#L208\n[2] https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.12.107/source/drivers/watchdog/watchdog_pretimeout.c#L113",
      "parentUuid": "fab4bd8c_42a9b044",
      "revId": "bb176d5b38e3ab0dce900adcc303dfea8a131fa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
